WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.240
 I wanted to do this because
I felt like the only thing

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.720
that I could speak very
passionately about is empathy.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.310
You start to realize
that there's more

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:11.790
going on in the world
than you and your dreams.

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:13.380
This class will
help you learn how

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:17.070
to harness the power of
empathy to transform everything

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.283
that you do and
everything that you are.

00:00:19.283 --> 00:00:20.700
This is one of
those subjects that

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.950
just needs many
people to come in here

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:26.460
and talk about it from
their point of view.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:31.670
Empathy's greatest potential
is equality for all mankind.

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:34.010
We've never seen that.

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:36.810
We don't know what
that looks like.

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:39.400
It's never happened.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.650
CORNEL WEST: What does
it mean to imagine

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:46.030
what it's like to get inside
of the skin of other people?

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:48.910
It's connected to a way
of life, and empathy

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:50.350
is at the center of it.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:51.850
GLORIA STEINEM:
Empathy allows us

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:58.377
to overcome the labels of race
and gender and caste and class.

00:00:58.377 --> 00:01:00.460
WALTER MOSLEY: You can't
have art without empathy.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:04.300
If you don't understand
the physical and emotional

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:09.062
experience of other creatures,
then your art's gonna be dead.

00:01:09.062 --> 00:01:11.020
ROBIN ARZÓN: It feels
good to achieve together.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.270
Even in the most
individualized sport,

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:15.827
empathy will allow
for connection.

00:01:15.827 --> 00:01:17.410
ROBERT REFFKIN:
Empathy is most needed

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:20.710
when it comes to
leading and recognizing

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:22.898
that not all people are you.

00:01:22.898 --> 00:01:24.940
ROXANE GAY: A lot of us
are very set in our ways.

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:26.710
We think what we think, and
we're not really interested

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:27.820
in outside points of view.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:31.300
But you don't really ever
grow when you allow yourself

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.683
to be constrained like that.

00:01:33.683 --> 00:01:35.350
PHARRELL WILLIAMS:
Curiosity and empathy

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:37.990
are the tools that I use when
I'm working in the studio.

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:41.770
Curiosity is the vehicle that
gets you to a place of empathy.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:44.230
The day that you put a
lid on your curiosity

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:46.690
is the day that you die.

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:47.860
 I'm Robin Arzón.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:49.150
 I'm Roxane Gay.

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:50.470
 I'm Walter Mosley.

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:51.790
 I'm Robert Reffkin.

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:53.200
 I'm Gloria Steinem.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.360
 I am Cornel West.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.950
 I'm Pharrell Williams,
and this is MasterClass.

